    "original_title": "Somalia: US confirms troop exit from Baledogle airfield over runway repair costs",
    "slug": "us-confirms-troop-withdrawal-from-baledogle-airfield-in-somalia",
    "summary": "The United States has withdrawn its troops from Baledogle Military Airfield in southern Somalia, citing the deteriorating runway as the reason. Most personnel have been moved to Mogadishu, according to AFRICOM.",
    "content": "The United States military has confirmed that it has pulled its forces out of Baledogle Military Airfield in southern Somalia. The decision was described by a senior officer as an economic one, driven by the runway's worsening condition, which had made it unsafe for aircraft operations. Army Lieutenant General John Brennan, deputy commander of US Africa Command (AFRICOM), stated that the majority of personnel previously stationed at Baledogle have been relocated to the capital, Mogadishu. \"The relocation from Baledogle was 100% an economic decision,\" Brennan told reporters. \"The surface of the airfield was declining to the point where it was becoming unsafe.\" The airfield, located in the Lower Shabelle region, had been used by US forces for various operations, including training and support missions.\n\nBaledogle airfield has been a significant base for US military activities in Somalia for several years. It was used as a staging ground for counterterrorism operations against al-Shabab and as a training site for Somali special forces. The US has maintained a presence in Somalia to support the federal government in its fight against the militant group. The withdrawal comes amid a broader reassessment of US military posture in the region. In recent years, the US has reduced its troop numbers in Somalia, particularly after the previous administration ordered a withdrawal in 2020, though some forces were later redeployed.\n\nThe move is attributed solely to the airfield's deteriorating infrastructure, not to any change in security policy. General Brennan emphasized that the decision was based on cost considerations related to repairing the runway. \"The surface of the airfield was declining to the point where it was becoming unsafe,\" he said, indicating that maintaining the facility had become economically unfeasible. The US military has not disclosed the exact number of troops affected or the specific costs involved in repairing the runway. However, the relocation to Mogadishu suggests that US operations in Somalia will continue from other locations.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the withdrawal may raise questions about the US commitment to security in Somalia. Many diaspora members have family in Somalia and are concerned about the stability of the region. The US has been a key partner in supporting the Somali government and African Union peacekeeping efforts. The relocation of troops to Mogadishu could signal a consolidation of US forces in the capital, potentially leading to a more focused approach in supporting Somali security forces. However, some diaspora members may worry that the withdrawal from Baledogle could create a security vacuum in the Lower Shabelle region, where al-Shabab remains active.\n\nThe Somali government has not yet commented on the US withdrawal. The airfield at Baledogle is located about 90 kilometers northwest of Mogadishu and has been a strategic location for military operations. The US Africa Command has stated that it remains committed to supporting Somali forces and that the relocation is a temporary measure until the runway can be repaired or alternative solutions are found. The economic rationale behind the decision underscores the challenges of maintaining remote military installations in austere environments.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-us-confirms-troop-exit-baledogle-airfield-runway-costs/",
