    "summary": "Torrential rain and strong winds in Dadaab, Kenya, have destroyed shelters and swept away possessions, leaving many Somali refugee families without food or shelter. Some have sought refuge with relatives, while others remain exposed.",
    "content": "Severe weather struck the Dadaab refugee complex in northeastern Kenya on 29 October, with heavy rain and powerful winds causing extensive damage to homes and belongings. The storms particularly affected the outskirts of Hagadera camp, where many Somali refugee families reside. Faisa Mohamed Hassan, a mother of seven, reported that her shelter in the Macalin Farah area collapsed and floodwaters carried away all her household items. She and her children, the eldest aged 10, are now staying with her mother’s family.\n\nDadaab, established in the early 1990s, is one of the world’s largest refugee complexes and primarily houses Somali refugees who have fled decades of conflict and drought. The camp has long faced challenges such as overcrowding, limited resources, and vulnerability to natural disasters. The recent storms add to the hardships of residents, many of whom rely on humanitarian aid for basic necessities.\n\nThe destruction of homes and loss of possessions have left affected families in a precarious situation. While some have found temporary shelter with relatives, others have no place to turn for food or protection from the rain. The United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR) and partner organizations are assessing the extent of the damage and mobilizing response efforts, but access and resources remain constrained.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the news of renewed suffering in Dadaab resonates deeply. Many diaspora members have family ties to the camp or were once residents themselves. The diaspora often plays a crucial role in supporting relatives through remittances and advocacy, and this event underscores the ongoing need for international support and durable solutions for Somali refugees.\n\nAs climate-related disasters become more frequent, the resilience of refugee communities in Dadaab is further tested. The international community is called upon to address both immediate humanitarian needs and long-term strategies to mitigate the impact of such events. The situation remains fluid, with assessments ongoing and needs expected to rise.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/11/downpours-destroy-dadaab-refugees-homes-and-belongings/",
